// Which pixels screen-space reflections may write at all. ONE statement, read by the trace (SSR.shader) and by
// the tile classification that decides where the trace, reconstruction and composite run
// (SSR.shader's classify half): if the two disagreed, a pixel the trace would reflect could sit in a tile nobody
// draws, and its reflection would silently vanish.

// UE GetRoughnessFade (ScreenSpaceReflections.usf) with its default ScreenSpaceReflectionMaxRoughness 0.6: full SSR
// up to half the max roughness, none at it. The trace samples the whole GGX lobe (SSR.shader), so a glossy surface
// reflects with its real blur; past the max the reflection is the environment's alone (the captures, the sky),
// which is what a lobe that wide sees anyway. A soft gate, not a metal-only cutoff: a smooth dielectric floor
// still gets its Fresnel reflection.
const float kSSRMaxRoughness = 0.6;
float SSRRoughnessFade(float roughness)
{
	return clamp(roughness * (-2.0 / kSSRMaxRoughness) + 2.0, 0.0, 1.0);
}

// The trace's own early-outs that depend on the G-buffer alone: sky (no normal) or too rough to reflect.
// A pixel failing this is written 0 by the trace.
bool SSRPixelCanReflect(vec4 gbufferNormal)
{
	return dot(gbufferNormal.rgb, gbufferNormal.rgb) > 0.001 && SSRRoughnessFade(gbufferNormal.a) >= 0.01;
}
